<p>
  Approvals have their own menu, because many kinds of documents go through them: expense claims
  and leave requests today, and more modules as they join (change requests, journal postings…). Everything
  works the same way whatever the document.
</p>

<h2>Inbox</h2>
<p>
  <.link navigate="/approvals">Approvals → Inbox</.link>
  lists everything waiting for <strong>your</strong>
  decision, from every module, oldest first. Open an item to approve or reject it on the document
  itself; rejecting needs a comment.
</p>

<h2>Change requests (maker-checker)</h2>
<p>
  For sensitive data, admins can require a second pair of eyes: in <.link navigate="/settings/workspace">Settings → Workspace</.link>, turn on
  <strong>Maker-checker</strong>
  and tick the areas it covers (employees, contacts &amp; companies, products &amp; prices, chart of
  accounts, tax rates, journal postings). From then on, saving a change in those areas doesn't apply it: it becomes a
  <strong>change request</strong>
  (<code>CR-…</code>) showing each field's current and proposed value.
</p>
<p>
  Another admin (or whoever your <em>Change requests</em>
  workflow names) approves it from the inbox or <.link navigate="/approvals/changes">Approvals → Change requests</.link>, and the change is applied
  as if the maker had saved it. A rejected change is never applied, and the maker can withdraw a
  change while it's waiting.
</p>
<p>
  With <strong>Journal postings</strong>
  ticked, manual journal entries and expenses recorded in Accounting are only posted to the ledger
  once a checker approves them. Postings made automatically by invoices, bills, payments and stock
  movements are not held.
</p>
<.callout kind={:warning}>
  You need at least two admins (or a checker workflow): nobody checks their own change.
</.callout>

<h2>History</h2>
<p>
  <.link navigate="/approvals/history">History</.link>
  shows every approval in the workspace, filtered by outcome and document type. Click one to see
  each step: who decided, when, and what they said.
</p>

<h2>Workflows</h2>
<p>
  Admins set up <.link navigate="/approvals/workflows">workflows</.link>
  per document type: <strong>initiator → approver 1 → … → final approver</strong>. Each approver is
  the initiator's manager, a specific person, anyone in an approval group, or any workspace admin.
  Use <em>Applies from</em>
  to route larger amounts (or longer leave, in days) through more people. Without a workflow, any
  admin approves expense claims, and the employee's manager (or an admin) approves leave.
</p>
<.callout>
  Nobody approves their own document. A step with nobody else to ask is skipped.
</.callout>

<h2>Approval groups</h2>
<p>
  <.link navigate="/approvals/groups">Approval groups</.link>
  (Finance, Management…) let any one of their members approve a step.
</p>
